1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,701
Welcome to the data sandbox. Currently,
the system is operating in 'Naive Graph'

2
00:00:05,701 --> 00:00:11,186
mode. We have no strict ontological
rules—just nodes and relationships. Let’s

3
00:00:11,186 --> 00:00:14,000
model a typical manufacturing scenario.

4
00:00:14,029 --> 00:00:19,652
We have a vehicle. Within it, there is a
suspension slot, where part "M zero one"

5
00:00:19,652 --> 00:00:24,361
is installed. Everything is
straightforward. But what happens when a

6
00:00:24,361 --> 00:00:26,189
real-world problem occurs?

7
00:00:26,230 --> 00:00:32,184
During an inspection, a two-millimetre
crack is discovered on part "M zero one".

8
00:00:32,184 --> 00:00:35,350
How do we store this fact in a naive
graph?

9
00:00:35,322 --> 00:00:40,485
The developer faces a dilemma. If we add
the property "crack: two millimetres"

10
00:00:40,485 --> 00:00:45,715
directly to the part node, then when we
try to query the car's condition a year

11
00:00:45,715 --> 00:00:50,878
ago, the system will answer that the part
was already broken. Because the node

12
00:00:50,878 --> 00:00:53,962
exists outside of time—it has been
overwritten.

13
00:00:53,923 --> 00:00:59,807
The obvious workaround is to attach the
defect information to the temporal edge:

14
00:00:59,807 --> 00:01:05,095
"MOUNTED_IN". Excellent. The crack is
anchored in time. The inspector is

15
00:01:05,095 --> 00:01:09,043
satisfied, and the database has saved the
information.

16
00:01:08,997 --> 00:01:14,256
But then comes the next day. The part is
removed from the vehicle and sent to the

17
00:01:14,256 --> 00:01:15,637
warehouse for repair.

18
00:01:15,607 --> 00:01:21,341
The mounting edge is closed and archived.
The part is now linked to the warehouse.

19
00:01:21,341 --> 00:01:26,438
Now, let’s ask the system: "How many
defective parts are currently in the

20
00:01:26,438 --> 00:01:27,287
warehouse?".

21
00:01:27,259 --> 00:01:32,747
The system answers: Zero. The crack has
disappeared from the vehicle, but in the

22
00:01:32,747 --> 00:01:38,097
warehouse, the part is registered as
completely flawless! The defect attribute

23
00:01:38,097 --> 00:01:43,099
remains buried in the archived mounting
edge. Our naive approach has just

24
00:01:43,099 --> 00:01:45,739
destroyed critical data about a fault.

25
00:01:45,703 --> 00:01:51,206
This is exactly where the naive approach
collapses, and an industrial ontology is

26
00:01:51,206 --> 00:01:56,503
required. We are now switching the system
to ISO fifteen nine twenty-six mode.

27
00:01:56,467 --> 00:02:01,568
Under this standard, a part is not a
static node, but a four-dimensional

28
00:02:01,568 --> 00:02:06,669
object with temporal life stages. A
defect is an independent entity that

29
00:02:06,669 --> 00:02:12,057
classifies the condition of a specific
temporal stage of the part. Now, when

30
00:02:12,057 --> 00:02:17,445
moved to the warehouse, the part takes
its state history with it. Data is no

31
00:02:17,445 --> 00:02:18,307
longer lost.

